    <Label Name="Title" Style="Heading" Text="Square Around Four Points" Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="16" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
    <Label Name="Description" Style="Explanation" Text="A, B, C and D sit on the four sides of a square, one on each side, in order around it. The square itself is gone. Here is how to get it back from the points alone.\n\nDraw AC. Through D draw the perpendicular to AC and mark F on it so that DF = AC (the dashed circle finds it). The line BF is one side of the square. The parallel to it through D is the opposite side, and the perpendiculars through A and C are the other two. EGHI is the square.\n\nWhy it works: AC goes from the left side to the right side, so across the square it spans exactly one side length. Turned a quarter turn, it spans one side length up and down instead: from the bottom side, where D is, it reaches exactly the top side, where B is.\n\nDrag the points. The circle crosses the perpendicular twice and the drawing always takes the same crossing, so it works for nice points, not for all." Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="64" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
  </Figures>
</Drawing>